Z||000003||cardealer||11-12-2001||08:36 PM||||sounds to me your either not getting proper atomizing (spelling?) with your n20 and fuel. the n2o and fuel are not mixing well enough and being dispursed equally through out your intake. i suggest, check your fresh air intake, make sure your are getting plenty of air flow. there are plenty more variables involed but maybe this will get you started.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shaun41178||11-12-2001||09:19 PM||||First of all you should not be using platinum plugs.<P>And second bottle pressure has nothing to do with it.  <P>Third the nitrous does not go bad in the cold weather.  Its in a sealed bottle.<P>Its my belief that the plug got a bit too much nitrous and not enough fuel.  It wasn't distributed evenly in the intake. <P>This has happened to me on my car too.  Plug 2 would always be the first one to go in my car.  Sometimes it would be fine and then other times it would just burn up running the same amount of nitrous.  The intake for that cylinder could just flow a bit more then the others causing it to go lean.<P>I wouldn't worry about it too much unless it starts happening all the time.  If so then go with a slightly larger fuel jet.<P>You have to remember that with the air getting colder it is getting denser as well so you will need to add a bit more fuel to keep from running lean and burning plugs.  I burned most of my plugs in colder weather.  i also used stock heat range plugs instead of going with cooler plugs.  <P>Using platinum plugs could get expensive especially if you keep burning the electrode off.  Go with stock plugs.  Cheaper and work just as well.  Plus you shouldnt' be using platinum with nitrous to begin with.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000005||NOS3800||11-12-2001||10:53 PM||||OK&lt; so i shouldn't use the platinum plugs. But what is causing the surging i am getting? Could it be that the bottle is loosing pressure during the runs? I think it is maybe more then half full during the last two runs. It was 50 degrees during the runs. I am going to look for a leak after the NOS solenoid tommorow.<BR>Um, so in hot weather I can decrease the fuel jet and in cold weather incresae the fuel flow? Ok, sounds simple enough. Being that mine is a dry kit could there be two much in the intake(NOS and Dense Air)?||||reg||5
Z||000006||1FST2M6||11-13-2001||08:32 AM||||NO NO NO .. platinum plugs are a bad thing.  and if your bottles in the back it's rather warm anyway.. i took the heat sheild off the trunk just for a bit more heat. Shaun he's got a ZEX kit.. no fuel just bumped fuel pressure. try to find a set of NGK plugs.. i'm running UR5s and haven't wiped out a set yet... i replaced them a couple times in the gold car but they are still fine with the current set up. what ignition system do you have?  the surging may be having a dry kit should make any difference... shaun and i had them with no problems. surging may be air in the bottle.. not liquid n2o. did the people that filled the bottle have to turn the compressor on? or just crack the bottle and have enought pressure to fill your bottle?  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||11-13-2001||01:02 PM||||well you still might not be getting enough fuel if you fuel jet is too big.  It wont' create enough vacuum to close off your FPR to give the amount of fuel you need.  Mihgt want to try and get more fuel in the cylinders.  <P>And again ditch those plugs.  Don't use platinum.  Go with what Travis uses or go with GM ones.<P>The surging is probably from the misfire.  That would be my first guess.  From the pressure in your cylinders being too high for your ignition to ignite it creating a dead cylinder and throwing the engine out of balance and the next time around it could fire the mixture which is the surging you feel.  <P>What kind of ignition system do you have?  If you still have the stock one then I would upgrade.  I would go with a MSD 6al unit or go with a Crane Hi-6 with ps91 coil.  You can get this Crane unit from Summit for about $150 and is the one I used.  Box and coil comes as a kit and it also includes a harness so you don't have to cut any wires to install it.  I had good results with this ignition.  If you want at tad bit more spark buy an extra coil.  Get a Ps-92 coil.  This is a race coil.  Throw that coil on at the track and take it off before you leave.  Use your 91 as your everyday coil.  Also get some low ohm wires.<P>If you are racing when outside temps are 50 degrees then your bottle pressure with it half full is going to be around 400 psi.  that is not good.  Won't have any power.  However it won't cause your surging.  However if there is air in it then that could cause the surging as nitrous is denser then air as you know and if air goes in it won't be as much power created as if nitrous went in.  Instead of getting a bottle heater put some bottles around it filled with hot water.  Then put a blanket over the bottles to keep the heat in.  Make sure the bottles are touching the nitrous bottle to transfer the heat better.  This method is much cheaper then getting a bottle heater for $100 or so.  But if you want to spend the money then that is fine too.  I felt my way worked well enough to justify not getting a actual heater.<P>Also what are your plugs gapped at?  You ask if they are gapped too far but don't say what they are at.  With a stock ignition I would keep it around .035-.040.  With my ignition I was running my plugs gapped at .050 and the Crane was able to fire it.  Couldnt' at .055 though.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000008||NOS3800||11-13-2001||06:28 PM||||Shaun and 1FST2M6 , you guys have been alot of help, thanks. The 3800 runs platinum plugs from the factory, thats why I have been using them. I will check into NGK's to replace the AUTOLITES 103's I was using(not champions like I stated before). I think I have found the surging problem. <BR>I held the NOS nozzle in the intake tube pointed toward the rear of the car and manually fired the NOS. It sprayed a steady stream for 13 seconds(1 quarter mile run) with no problems. This was with only 600 pounds of pressure. But when I looked at the ZEX nitrous managment unit which contains both solenoids and I guess a computer chip, it was COMPLETELY iced over! But I figured that was normal since it was 48 degrees outside. So I did another "run" and watched the unit. Something is definetly wrong inside the box! It looked like liquid was leaking from the input NOS line and output NOS line at the box, not the lines. The liquid quickly FROZE the box completley. You could hear it crackling. I called ZEX and told them I had a leak. The guy asked, was it leaking all the time or just when it was sprayed. I told him only when it sprays. His exact words were"OH S**T". So I am sending them the unit for repairs. I hope the freezing of the box may have screwed up the fuel management which caused the fried spark plug. I had the AutoLites gapped at .053, do you think thats ok? The rest of the plugs looked great. <BR>Also do you guys use an octane booster or just get some race fuel when at the track? My experience with the octane boost hasn't been favorable.||||reg||1
